I ascended a fasting Monk. Since I haven't found a YAAP describing
how to do it, I thought I'd let you know how I did it. (Lots of
spoilers below!) By fasting, I mean I never used the 'e' command to
eat anything, although I did quaff. I just prayed when weak or fainting
to get a full stomach. Fasting is a fun challenge to tackle when you
think you've about got the game figured out.

I didn't try to do anything more challenging than fasting, except I
did remain weaponless (if you don't count accidentally hitting with
my pickaxe (why doesn't it ask if I really meant to do that?)). I
wished, polypiled, and genocided as needed, and I just paid off the
demons.

You play a fasting character in three distinct phases. Phase 1 lasts
until you get slow digestion, and it is the hardest part of the game
because even if you play perfectly, you will probably die.

I tried several games, and I always ended up killed while fainted
before I was able to reconcile with my god after a few thousand turns.
I think you would have to be fairly lucky to live past about 20,000
turns without a ring of slow digestion, although finding an altar on
about the first five levels helps, and I think you could last longer
than that with good use of an icebox. So to win, you will probably need
to have a =3DoSD by about turn 15K to 20K. I always hoped for a magic
lamp in minetown to get the ring, because even if you are lucky enough
to find a =3DoSD, it's tough to identify it in time. You will certainly
starve if you wear rings for more than a couple turns, so identifying
=3DoSD is hard. You have to do it with scrolls, because casting identify
would make you too hungry.

So, on about my 10th try, I found an altar on level 4. Altars are good
because sacrificing shortens your praying timeout. I hung around the
altar until I felt strong enough for mine town. (Typing '222.' is
my favorite way to kill time.) I headed to minetown to see if anyone
had a magic lamp for me. No lamp, but I did find an icebox in a store.
Hmmm. I emptied the icebox in the store and then bought it for about
$50 and dragged it to the stairs. Too heavy to drag it up. OK, drop
everything else, drag the icebox up, then head down to get my stuff.
Repeat until you get back to the altar. Dragging an icebox too far
between feedings would probably leave me too hungry and make me dead,
but I did get it back to level four. My suggestion is to sacrifice
until reconciled, then drag the icebox a few levels, then back to the
altar for your feeding.

Here's how I used the icebox (although I'm sure it is possible to
win without one): Leave the icebox a knight's move away from the
altar so it is nice and close but you can still zap teleport if you get
in trouble without sending your loaded icebox to who-knows-where. (I
always do my waiting while standing right on the altar.) Sacrifice
everything that you can until you are reconciled. Then put every
sacrificeable corpse into the icebox until you are almost fainting,
then pray to get fed. If you get hungry before you can safely pray,
take corpses from the icebox and sacrifice them. Note that good corpses
are heavy, and hauling heavy stuff makes you hungrier. This strategy
should give you a better chance of reconciling before you starve.

This little plan was working just fine, until I had a long prayer
timeout and couldn't sacrifice when I was hungry. "I've got to be
almost reconciled," Thor thought to himself, so he sat on the altar
for 222 moves until he felt weak. Then back and forth between the
icebox and the altar, but still no reconciliation until he was
fainting. "Ooh! Here comes an owlbear! I bet sacrificing him will do
the trick!" But Thor fainted and the owlbear killed him.

But a couple of games later I finally found my magic lamp in minetown!
Now to find an altar. Thor got lucky and found his familiar altar on
level 4, with a loaded icebox right there and his former self's grave
right close by. The magic lamp got me my =3DoSD, and it's on to phase 2
of the game! Life is good when you have SD and you haven't been
crowned yet.

Once I had SD, I was able to pray occasionally for stuff other than a
full stomach. The SD let me go about 9000 moves between praying for
food and fainting again, as long as I wasn't burdened, I wore no
other rings, and didn't cast anything. I did wear an amulet of
reflection because I hate it when a stupid mordor orc gets lucky and
finds a wand of death. Later in the game I graduated to a shield of
reflection because I like to be stupid-proof by wearing an amulet of
lifesaving (although I never died), and a fasting monk can't cast
many spells anyway. When I did need to cast, I took off my shield and
my gauntlets of power, and swapped my cloak of MR for a robe. I hung
around altars, explored a little and prayed when I was confident that I
wouldn't need food for a long time. Eventually I got crowned, so I
gave up on praying for goodies, and moved on to phase 3.

After crowing, your praying timeout increases, so you can never safely
pray or offer unless you are weak. In phase 3, there isn't as much
point to hanging around an altar because you pray so seldom. I finally
had magic resistance, so I quickly explored down through most of
gehennom. I used the /oW from the castle once to get my speed boots,
then finally headed back and finished the quest.

I lost my =3DoSD once to a water nymph. So I pulled my spare ring from my
bag, slipped my towel around my head, and pummeled that nymph to a
pulp.

I made two mistakes that almost killed me. The first was, even after I
had the quest artifact which tells me when I can safely pray, I got
hungry after finding the vibrating square. I headed back up to level 23
because I always liked to pray at an altar in the hope that I would get
something good like protection in addition to the full belly. I got to
the altar and rather than using enlightenment to see if it was safe, I
killed an animal and sacrificed it. Poof! Here's Magicbane! But now I
can't pray and I just got weak! I pulled my potions of juice from my
bag and quaffed a couple and it took me from weak to hungry. To
survive, I've got to get in good with my god quickly. It may not have
been the best route to take, but I used my third wish to get a wand of
create monster. Have you ever wished for one of those? Zap, sacrifice,
repeat, then recharge, then recharge again. I made it to reconciliation
without fainting, so I prayed, got my free lunch, then back to work.

I located the vibrating square, killed all the critters guarding
Rodney, headed back to my altar, took off my ring, got weak, then
prayed. Do you think I can kill Rodney, get the amulet, and get back
here before getting hungry? Well, there's always Vlad's tower.

I was still wimpier than I usually am at the end game, about 195 HP
because of limited praying, but I was tired of waiting and decided to
go for the finish. I killed Rodney, got the amulet, and headed back up
no problem. I passed my altar on level 23 and kept going because I
wasn't hungry yet. I figured I would pray at the altar on level four
to head into the planes on a full stomach. But I was tired and worried
about Rodney and somehow forgot to wait and pray at my last altar. I
headed up to the plane of earth and then I remembered that I would be
hungry any time now. I figured I had lost the game with that stupid
move, but I still had some juice in my bag and had two wands to get
more. Maybe I could survive.

I made it to the astral plane.without getting hungry. I don't know
the trick for figuring out which is the correct altar from a distance,
so I headed toward Pestilence and hoped I wouldn't have to mess with
Famine. I had mostly done without Conflict because it makes me hungry,
but I couldn't keep up with all the scrolls and wands of create
monster that everyone kept using and all the "Monsters appear from
nowhere!, so I slipped it on and got hungry almost immediately. I
removed conflict and pulled out a couple of wands of teleportation.
They make nice tunnels in crowds of monsters, so I hurried through into
the temple. I pulled out my wand of create monster (the one I had used
a wish on) and summoned a troll who immediate got scared and fled. This
is the right temple! So I sacrificed and finished without eating
anything. It can be done!

To summarize, these are the problems with fasting:
=B7	Since I couldn't pray much, I didn't get good HP.
=B7	Couldn't eat a lizard, so stoning was always a threat. Keep a
potion of acid.
=B7	Don't wear any rings for long other than SD. Conflict seems to
make you hungry very quickly, so I didn't use it much. Levitation
didn't seem to be much of a problem.
=B7	Praying won't get you blessings as often, especially early in the
game, because you have to save your prayers until you are weak.
=B7	No praying for food in Gehennom, except in Vlad's tower.
=B7	If you ever anger your god, you are likely dead meat.

